# Authorization

Wyrd has two authorization layers: RBAC resolves what a principal's roles
permit, and the `PolicyHook` extension gates the `/v1/authz/check` endpoint for
agent delegation. On an authz-check request both run, in that order.

For how a caller obtains a token in the first place, see
[Authentication](/concepts/authentication/); for the whole identity picture, see
[Identity and auth](/concepts/identity-and-auth/).

## RBAC

A `Permission` is `resource:action`. Roles carry a JSONB set of permissions;
role claims are embedded in the token at issuance and resolved against the
database at verify time.

The resolution path:
1. Token carries a list of role names in its claims.
2. On verify, `SqlPermissionResolver.roles_by_name` fetches the permission sets
   for those role names from the tenant's role table.
3. `effective_permissions` merges the sets, applying wildcard (`resource:*`) and
   `AnyOf` subsumption. A role with `cards:*` subsumes `cards:read`, `cards:write`,
   and every other `cards` action.

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Built-in role', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Permissions' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['admin', 'All resources and actions (*)'],
    ['writer', 'cards:read, cards:write, artifacts:read, artifacts:write, operators:invoke, evals:run, triggers:write'],
    ['reader', 'cards:read, artifacts:read, audit:read'],
    ['agent', 'cards:read, cards:write, artifacts:read, artifacts:write, operators:invoke, evals:run'],
    ['runtime_admin', 'service_accounts:write, delegation:issue']
  ]}
/>

Built-in roles are seeded per tenant at tenant creation.

## Two "kind" concepts

Two distinct "kind" fields appear in auth that are easy to confuse:

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Field', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Type' },
    { header: 'What it means' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['PrincipalKind', 'User | Service { card_ref } | Agent { card_ref }', 'The identity of the token holder: who is acting.'],
    ['PrincipalKindPolicy', 'Human | Workload', 'Per-issuer config: whether an OIDC issuer is for human login or machine workloads.']
  ]}
/>

`PrincipalKind` is encoded in the token and resolved on every verify.
`PrincipalKindPolicy` is checked once during token issuance to ensure a
workload assertion is not used for human login and vice versa.

## Delegation

Agent-to-agent calls run under a **delegated token**: an access token one
principal mints on behalf of another Service or Agent. The caller exchanges its
own token via the `token-exchange` grant on `POST /auth/token` and must hold the
`delegation:issue` permission. The minted token carries an `act` chain, the
ordered record of who delegated to whom, and no refresh token. Delegation depth is
capped at 5.

<WyrdSequenceDiagram
  title="Delegation (token-exchange)"
  participants={['Caller', 'Wyrd server']}
  messages={[
    { from: 'Caller', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'POST /auth/token · token-exchange · requested_subject' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'verify caller · require delegation:issue' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'resolve requested Service/Agent (server-owned)' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Caller', label: 'delegated token · act chain · depth ≤ 5 · no refresh', dashed: true }
  ]}
/>

Delegation is a preview grant: it is enabled by `WYRD_AUTH_ALLOW_PREVIEW` and is
rejected under a production profile until preview auth is cleared. Delegated
tokens are the only tokens `/v1/authz/check` accepts.

## Authz extension (authz/check)

`POST /v1/authz/check` is Wyrd's external authorization endpoint. A caller, or a
service mesh acting for it, asks one question: *may this delegated principal
perform this action on this target?* Wyrd answers by running **your** policy code
(`PolicyHook`) and then its built-in RBAC. This is the seam that lets a team run
its own policy engine (OPA, Cedar, a hand-written rule set) inside Wyrd's
identity and delegation model without forking the server.

Two things make it useful. Wyrd has already verified the token, enforced tenant
isolation, confirmed the call is a **delegated** Service/Agent call, and resolved
the full delegation chain before your hook runs, so your policy receives typed,
trustworthy identity rather than a raw JWT to re-parse. And the endpoint speaks a
fixed JSON contract, so a service mesh can call it as an external authorization
provider for every inter-service hop.

Only **delegated** tokens reach it: tokens issued via `token-exchange` that
carry a non-empty `act` chain. `guard_reason` rejects User tokens, direct
(non-delegated) Service/Agent tokens, and card-less tokens with a `403` before
any policy runs.

### The two layers

Every authz-check request passes through two gates, in this order. **Both must
pass.**

1. **`PolicyHook` (your code) runs first.** It returns `Allow` or
   `Deny { reason }`. A `Deny` short-circuits, and RBAC is not consulted.
2. **RBAC runs second, only if the hook allowed.** Wyrd checks the callee's
   `effective_permissions` against the requested `action`.

A hook `Allow` does not bypass RBAC. It means "my policy permits this; now apply
the principal's role permissions." Use the hook for attribute- and context-based
rules (this caller, this target, this time, this delegation chain); leave coarse
`resource:action` grants to RBAC.

<WyrdSequenceDiagram
  title="authz/check evaluation"
  participants={['Caller / mesh', 'Wyrd server', 'PolicyHook']}
  messages={[
    { from: 'Caller / mesh', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'POST /v1/authz/check · delegated token · X-Original-*' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'verify · guard delegated · build AuthzCheckContext' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'PolicyHook', label: 'evaluate(ctx)' },
    { from: 'PolicyHook', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'Allow / Deny(reason)', dashed: true },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'RBAC permission_check (only if hook allowed)' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Caller / mesh', label: '200 allow · 200 missing_permission · 403 denied', dashed: true }
  ]}
/>

### The request

`POST /v1/authz/check`

Headers:

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Header', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Required' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['X-Wyrd-Access-Token', 'Yes', 'Bearer <access-token>. Must be a delegated Service or Agent token (minted through token-exchange with a non-empty act chain). User tokens, direct Service/Agent tokens, and card-less tokens are rejected with 403 before any policy runs.'],
    ['Content-Type', 'Yes', 'application/json.'],
    ['X-Original-Method / -Path / -Host', 'No', 'The original request line, projected by a service mesh. Surfaced to your hook as ctx.metadata. All-or-nothing: if any of the three is missing, ctx.metadata is None, which is not an error.']
  ]}
/>

Body:

```json
{
  "target": { "kind": "Service", "name": "billing", "version": "1.2.0", "space": "prod" },
  "action": "card_write",
  "context": { "amount_cents": 5000 }
}
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Field', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Type' },
    { header: 'Required' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['target', 'CardRef', 'Yes', 'The card the action targets: { kind, name, version, space }.'],
    ['action', 'string', 'Yes', 'The well-known value "card_write", or any valid resource:action permission identifier. Drives the RBAC layer.'],
    ['context', 'object', 'No', 'Arbitrary JSON, passed verbatim to your hook as ctx.request.context. Defaults to null when omitted. Unknown top-level fields on the body itself are rejected.']
  ]}
/>

```bash
curl -sS -X POST https://wyrd.internal/v1/authz/check \
  -H 'X-Wyrd-Access-Token: Bearer <delegated-access-token>' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
        "target": {"kind": "Service", "name": "billing", "version": "1.2.0", "space": "prod"},
        "action": "card_write",
        "context": {"amount_cents": 5000}
      }'
```

### The response

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Outcome', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Status' },
    { header: 'Body' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['Allow', '200', '{ "decision": "allow" }'],
    ['RBAC miss', '200', '{ "decision": "deny", "reason": "missing_permission", "required": "<permission>" }'],
    ['Policy deny', '403', 'Wyrd error WYRD_AUTHZ_403_POLICY_DENIED; details.reason is the string your hook returned.'],
    ['Not a delegated token', '403', 'Wyrd error WYRD_AUTHZ_403_REQUIRES_DELEGATED_TOKEN.'],
    ['Unknown action', '400', 'Wyrd validation error; action was neither "card_write" nor a valid permission.'],
    ['Missing / malformed token', '401 / 400', 'Unauthenticated (no header) or BadTokenFormat (not a Bearer credential).']
  ]}
/>

The distinction between the two `200` cases matters: **an RBAC miss is a `200`,
not a `403`.** The token is valid and the request was well-formed; the callee
simply lacks the permission, so the answer is a normal "deny" decision. Only a
`PolicyHook` denial, an explicit policy verdict, is a `403`:

```json
{
  "code": "WYRD_AUTHZ_403_POLICY_DENIED",
  "status": 403,
  "message": "policy denied invoke",
  "details": { "reason": "cross-space delegated write is not allowed" }
}
```

### What your hook receives

Wyrd builds an `AuthzCheckContext` from the verified token and the request body,
then passes it to `PolicyHook::evaluate`. This is the entire input to your policy;
there is no callback to fetch more identity.

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Field', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Type' },
    { header: 'What it carries' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['callee', 'Principal', 'The principal the call runs as: id, kind (Service/Agent with its card_ref), tenant_id, roles, and effective_permissions.'],
    ['caller', 'PrincipalRef', 'The immediate delegator for this hop.'],
    ['chain', 'Vec<PrincipalRef>', 'The initiator-first delegation chain. The callee is not included.'],
    ['request', 'AuthzCheckRequest', 'The target, action, and context from the request body.'],
    ['metadata', 'Option<...>', 'The mesh-projected method / path / host, when the X-Original-* headers were present.'],
    ['request_id', 'RequestId', 'Correlator for audit records and response headers.']
  ]}
/>

### Implementing a PolicyHook

`PolicyHook` lives in the `wyrd-auth-check` crate. It is one async method plus a
marker that Wyrd uses to refuse the shipped stub in production.

```rust
#[async_trait]
pub trait PolicyHook: Send + Sync {
    async fn evaluate(&self, ctx: &AuthzCheckContext) -> PolicyDecision;

    /// Whether this is the placeholder default (defaults to false).
    fn is_stub_default(&self) -> bool { false }
}
```

`PolicyDecision` is `Allow` or `Deny { reason }`; the reason is surfaced to the
caller in the `403` body. Implement `evaluate` with your policy logic: call OPA or
Cedar, evaluate a rule set, or inspect the delegation chain directly. A real hook
leaves `is_stub_default` at its `false` default; that is what lets it pass the
production boot check.

```rust
use async_trait::async_trait;
use wyrd_auth_check::{AuthzCheckContext, PolicyHook};
use wyrd_runtime::PrincipalKind;
use wyrd_spec::card::policy::PolicyDecision;

/// Deny any delegated write whose target lives in a different space than the
/// callee's own card. Everything else falls through to RBAC.
#[derive(Debug)]
struct SameSpacePolicy;

#[async_trait]
impl PolicyHook for SameSpacePolicy {
    async fn evaluate(&self, ctx: &AuthzCheckContext) -> PolicyDecision {
        let callee_space = match &ctx.callee.kind {
            PrincipalKind::Service { card_ref } | PrincipalKind::Agent { card_ref } => {
                &card_ref.space
            }
            _ => return PolicyDecision::deny("callee is not a service or agent"),
        };

        if &ctx.request.target.space != callee_space {
            return PolicyDecision::deny("cross-space delegated write is not allowed");
        }
        PolicyDecision::Allow
    }
}
```

### Registering your hook

The hook is a **compile-time Rust seam, not a config file.** You cannot name a
policy engine in `server.toml` or an environment variable: a trait object can't
be deserialized. You install it by embedding the `wyrd-server` crate and calling
`with_policy_hook` on the assembled `AppState` before building the router.

Production also requires a real audit writer. Wyrd persists one audit fact per
authz-check decision through the `AuthzAuditWriter` trait; the shipped default is
a no-op.

```rust
// AppState is assembled during boot (Postgres pools, signing key, issuer
// resolvers). Install your policy plane before building the router; both
// installers are required for a production boot.
let state = state
    .with_policy_hook(Arc::new(SameSpacePolicy))
    .with_audit_writer(Arc::new(PgAuthzAuditWriter::new()));

// Fails closed under APP_ENV=staging|production if either is still the shipped
// stub, if the token verifier is missing, or if preview auth is still enabled.
state.production_validate()?;

let router = build_router(state);
```

<Aside title="The stock binary is dev-only for authz/check" type="caution">

The shipped `wyrd-server` binary mounts `StubAllowPolicyHook` (allows
everything) and `NoopAuthzAuditWriter` (writes nothing). Under a **development**
profile these defaults let authz/check work locally. Under a **production**
profile (`APP_ENV=staging` or `production`), `production_validate` rejects both
stubs and the server **refuses to boot** with `StubPolicyHook` and
`NoopAuditWriter` errors.

There is no configuration path that swaps in a real hook. To run authz/check in
production you must embed `wyrd-server` as a library, install your `PolicyHook`
and `AuthzAuditWriter`, and serve the resulting router. The `with_policy_hook` /
`with_audit_writer` installers and `production_validate` are the durable
contract; the surrounding boot helpers may change.

</Aside>

### Service-mesh integration

The optional `X-Original-Method`, `X-Original-Path`, and `X-Original-Host`
headers exist so a service mesh can call authz/check as an **external
authorization provider** (the ext_authz pattern). The mesh sidecar intercepts an
inter-service request, forwards the delegated token and the original request line
to `/v1/authz/check`, and allows or denies the hop based on the response. Your
hook sees the projected request line in `ctx.metadata` alongside the identity and
delegation chain.

Two integration styles hit the same endpoint. In the **direct** style, the calling
agent asks authz/check itself before making a delegated call: no mesh headers, and
`ctx.metadata` is `None`. In the **mesh-mediated** style, a sidecar calls
authz/check per hop with the `X-Original-*` headers projected, so `ctx.metadata`
carries the original method, path, and host.

Either way the token must be a delegated Service/Agent token, the same token that
would carry the call itself.

